<section class="panel" aria-label="Grade 3 Unit 8 Lesson 4 tiny-house project">
<h2>Tiny House: Design and Solve</h2>
<p><a href="https://im.kendallhunt.com/k5/teachers/grade-3/unit-8/lesson-4/lesson.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Original 2021 visual source with three design options</a>. Teacher checks original photos/diagrams; not reproduced here.</p>
<table class="source-chart"><thead><tr><th>Source design</th><th>Rectangular dimension</th><th>Area</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Shipping container</td><td>8 × 20 ft</td><td>160 ft², perimeter 56 ft</td></tr>
<tr><td>Cabin</td><td>Ground: 8 × 10 ft; upper: 8 × 8 ft</td><td>80 + 64 = 144 ft² across two floors</td></tr>
<tr><td>School bus</td><td>7 × 14 ft</td><td>98 ft², perimeter 42 ft</td></tr></tbody></table>
<p>Choose one house. Partition each relevant floor into rooms; include doors, windows and furniture. Labels must match real floor-area constraints. For the cabin, there is no single perimeter belonging to the 144 ft² sum across stories.</p>
<p>Write exactly two solvable questions about your OWN design: one area question and one perimeter question. Answer and revise with a partner, then exchange with a NEW partner.</p>
<p><strong>Teacher review:</strong> verify source figures, nonoverlapping spaces, units, design reasoning, accuracy and peer feedback. Gated publisher Cool-down is excluded.</p>
</section>
